From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dan Yocom <630234+danyocom@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 20:10:09 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07/14] Split the in-game reset dispatch into shared and
 per-dispatcher parts

Nearly every retail game uses the stock SDK interrupt dispatcher, but a few
developers replaced it with one of their own, and those games cannot be hooked
by a single signature. Prepare for that by separating the part that depends on
the dispatcher from the part that does not.

The key check, which counts the vblanks during which the reset keys are held,
moves into its own patch heap allocation and is shared. What remains per
dispatcher is only the replacement for its handler lookup, which is now found
through a table of known dispatcher versions.

The three parts agree on one convention: a dispatch part leaves in r2 the
address that carries on with normal dispatch, and both the key check and the
reset enter it the same way. The reset already worked like that, so it is
unchanged.

Behaviour for the stock dispatcher is the same, including the registers the
handler is entered with. The dispatch is two allocations instead of one, which
is easier rather than harder to place, since arm9 patch space is made of the
gaps between the secure area syscall thunks.
